Logic
The problem has a catch: every turn you wait, the remaining kids lose 1 happiness point. It's basically a "decay" factor.

Imagine this situation:-
You are Santa and you have k gifts to hand out. The children are lined up, sorted by how excited they are (Santa's Nice List, happiest in the front).
Every time you hand a gift to the child at the front, you "bank" their happiness score.
However, the children waiting in the queue get anxious (or cold!) wondering if they are next.
This uncertainty causes everyone still in line to lose 1 point of happiness per turn.
The Strategy:
To get the maximum total joy, you must "cash out" the happiest kids immediately. If you save a super-happy kid for last, the penalty (waiting time) might eat away all their joy before they even get the gift!

Approach:-
Sort Descending: happiness.sort(reverse=True). We line up the happiest kids first.
Loop k times: We only have k gifts to give.
The Math: For the child at index i, their current happiness is happiness[i] - i (Original Happiness - Time Waited).
No Negative Vibes: If happiness[i] - i drops below zero, we count it as 0. We don't add negative happiness, maybe those kids just wait for next Christmas (?)

Complexity:-
Time: O(NlogN) because sorting the line is the hardest part. Handing out the gifts is just O(k).
Space: O(1) effectively (ignoring sort memory).

Code:-
class Solution {
    public long maximumHappinessSum(int[] happiness, int k) {
        Arrays.sort(happiness);
        long ans = 0;
        int n = happiness.length - 1;
        for(int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            int val = happiness[n - i] - i;
            if(val > 0) ans += val;
            else break;
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
